#{[17592186533236 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533244 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533245 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533247 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-18 & 42 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 14\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533250 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533266 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533272 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533285 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533286 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533304 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 15\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533306 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533312 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533322 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 15\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533333 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 16 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533343 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-40 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533363 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 30 \\\\ -3 & -15 \\\\ 9 & 45\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533374 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533378 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}56 & -14 \\\\ 42 & 14\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533395 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533418 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533431 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-30 & 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533438 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533456 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-40 & 8 \\\\ 10 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533459 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533464 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533482 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 15 \\\\ -9 & 12\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533483 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}84 & -36 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 14 & -6\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533488 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 42 \\\\ -14 & 56\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533494 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533504 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}36 \\\\ -18 \\\\ 44\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533506 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}18 & -42 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -14\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533511 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533520 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 30 \\\\ 18 & 45 \\\\ 42 & 105\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533528 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 30 \\\\ 3 & 15 \\\\ 9 & 45\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533537 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533543 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533546 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533553 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533562 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533570 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533580 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533605 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 2 \\\\ 8 & -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533610 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533619 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533623 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533631 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533637 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 18 & 42 \\\\ 30 & -45 & -105\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533645 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533649 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533651 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -10 \\\\ -6 & 8\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533659 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533665 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533670 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-40 & 10 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533671 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -42 \\\\ 14 & 56\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533672 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -2 \\\\ -40 & 10\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533683 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533697 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533706 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533712 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533722 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533727 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533738 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-36 & 84 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 14\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533747 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533753 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533754 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533763 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 21 \\\\ -7 & 28\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186533977 :question/worked-solution "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is $\\lambda =4$ and it's positive, we have a $\\text{unstable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f6/f67469d62d6a0eb61397927924e55868437ad09f405c3b2a9d73567af00c4620.png)"] [17592186533986 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=3({y}^{T}Ax)$ where \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186533986 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}3{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}=3{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=3(y\\cdot {x}^{T}).$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=3(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5\\end{bmatrix})=3(\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 15\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 30 \\\\ -3 & -15 \\\\ 9 & 45\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186533992 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)=(det(X))^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186533992 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\\in {R}^{2\\times 2},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\nf(X)=(det(X))^{2}=({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2} & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot {x}_{22} & 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot (-{x}_{21}) \\\\ 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot (-{x}_{12}) & 2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\cdot {x}_{11}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2det(X)\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=2(4\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 3)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -42 \\\\ 14 & 56\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186533993 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(det(X)).\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186533993 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\\in {R}^{2\\times 2},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\nf(X)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(det(X))=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}).\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{-{x}_{22}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{{x}_{21}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} \\\\ \\frac{{x}_{12}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{-{x}_{11}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{-4-(-3)} & \\frac{1}{-4-(-3)} \\\\ \\frac{-3}{-4-(-3)} & -\\frac{(-1)}{-4-(-3)}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{-1} & \\frac{1}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{-3}{-1} & -\\frac{(-1)}{-1}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186533994 :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the second principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $13$ and $3.$"] [17592186533994 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=13$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 12\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186533999 :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $1$ and $6.$"] [17592186533999 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-6I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186534000 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)={y}^{T}Ax$ where \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186534000 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186534003 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(X)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{x}_{ij}^{2}\n$$\n\n \nwhere ${x}_{ij}$ are the corresponding matrix's entries. What is the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ at $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186534003 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of a function $f:{R}^{m\\times n}\\to R$ with respect to a matrix $X\\in {R}^{m\\times n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{mn}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(X)$ where $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\\in {R}^{2\\times 2},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\nf(X)={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{x}_{ij}^{2}=({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,{∇}_{X}f(X) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}({x}_{11}^{2}+{x}_{12}^{2}+{x}_{21}^{2}+{x}_{22}^{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{11} & 2{x}_{12} \\\\ 2{x}_{21} & 2{x}_{22}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11} & {x}_{12} \\\\ {x}_{21} & {x}_{22}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2X.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{X}f(X)=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot (1) & 2\\cdot (5) \\\\ 2\\cdot (-3) & 2\\cdot (4)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186534008 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 3}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=3({y}^{T}Ax)$ where \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186534008 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}3{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}=3{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=3(y\\cdot {x}^{T}).$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=3(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix})=3(\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 & 14 \\\\ 10 & -15 & -35\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 18 & 42 \\\\ 30 & -45 & -105\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186534012 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=1\\pm 4\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1,2}=[\\pm \\text{i},1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-1}} \\\\ y={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{16}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0.86$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $0.86$ | {{field-4}} |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/50/50b71bd4aefb94179b55a94992f9e7a42b9ddbd50cd3a9dcf17645f4afd4846c.png)\n\nAs $t\\to \\infty ,$ the curve\n{{field-5}}.\nSo, we have\n{{field-6}}\nthat is traversed \n{{field-7}}.\n\nThe spiral is traversed toward \n{{field-8}}\nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nThe {{field-9}} of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f6/f60bdf0ec9c734a1d0d352c448b2c829869ba9fe0115bd860f639b9e4dc83cd0.png)"] [17592186534012 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{16}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0.86$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $0.86$ | $1.48$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/50/50b71bd4aefb94179b55a94992f9e7a42b9ddbd50cd3a9dcf17645f4afd4846c.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)|\\le 1$ and $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)|\\le 1$ but ${e}^{t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, the curve $\\text{diverges}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we have $\\text{a spiral}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$\n\nAgain, since ${e}^{t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ the spiral is traversed toward $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nThe $\\text{phase portrait}$ of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f6/f60bdf0ec9c734a1d0d352c448b2c829869ba9fe0115bd860f639b9e4dc83cd0.png)"] [17592186534013 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-6$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-6t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-6t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={e}^{-6t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{-6t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-6t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/ff/ff337e0120343c7c956afa5e49ea3b482fbd79fe363783a018842d1bfeb340d6.png)"] [17592186534022 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=\\frac{1}{2}(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"8px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"45px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"45px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"8px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-35$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186534022 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b\\in {R}^{3}.$ Then, $L(A)=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y)=y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}={y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}={x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =(Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =(\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -70 \\\\ 0 & -35 \\\\ 0 & 63\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186534029 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)={y}^{T}Ax$ where \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186534029 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-40 & 10 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186534034 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=2({y}^{T}Ax)$ where \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186534034 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}2{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}=2{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=2(y{x}^{T}).$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=2(\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 7\\end{bmatrix})=2(\\begin{bmatrix}-18 & 42 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}-36 & 84 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 14\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186534045 :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $1,$ $2,$ and $9.$"] [17592186534045 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=9,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=9:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-9I & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-9\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-9I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186534047 :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the third principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $8,$ $5,$ and $2.$"] [17592186534047 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=8,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=5,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=3,$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186534071 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v=[1,\\text{ }-1]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{{field-1}}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}{{field-2}}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-2t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/45/4522e4e6ed382e821aaa47d40a191135161f95eef276b6e8ba3a8514e6bfdfd8.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y=(-t+1){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | {{field-7}} | $0$ | $0.18$ |\n| $y$ | {{field-8}} | $1$ | $0.18$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/db/dbf5a13ecd5e81c399204fd29d235e410fcb62274555cf3e5b32a383c1078d24.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/a6/a680b8c9319290df3beb20d7db964f385b368b01ead33961e2c10d22b19db4eb.png)"] [17592186534071 :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/45/4522e4e6ed382e821aaa47d40a191135161f95eef276b6e8ba3a8514e6bfdfd8.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ -t+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{-2t} \\\\ y=(-t+1){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-1.36$ | $0$ | $0.18$ |\n| $y$ | $4.08$ | $1$ | $0.18$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/db/dbf5a13ecd5e81c399204fd29d235e410fcb62274555cf3e5b32a383c1078d24.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/13/13a57f14ea65118473c24c6eb1dbf50a0461060201ced051bb2eb6776f5daf64.png)"] [17592186534073 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$20$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$36$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186534073 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b\\in {R}^{2}.$ Then, $L(A)={y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y)=2y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=2{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}={x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}{x}_{j}..\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $2$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=2y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =2y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =2(Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =2(\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 20 \\\\ -18 & 36\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
